= Poverty in India: Dimensions, Measurement & Policy Response

Poverty is a multidimensional state of deprivation in which an individual or household lacks the financial resources and essential capabilities required to attain a socially acceptable minimum standard of living.

== 1. Historical & Post-Independence Poverty Estimation
- *Pre-Independence Pioneer (Dadabhai Naoroji):* Naoroji was the first to estimate a poverty line in colonial India using his famous concept of the *"Jail Cost of Living"*. He used the dietary cost of feeding an adult prisoner in British jails, adjusting for children and dependent demographics, to arrive at an average subsistence poverty line.
- *Post-Independence Initiatives:*
  1. *Planning Commission Study Group (1962):* Formulated a minimum consumption expenditure benchmark of ₹20 per capita per month (₹25 for urban areas) at 1960–61 prices.
  2. *Task Force on Projections of Minimum Needs (1979):* Defined the national poverty line strictly in terms of nutritional *minimum daily calorie intake*:
     - *Rural Areas:* *2,400 calories* per person per day (higher due to arduous agricultural physical labour).
     - *Urban Areas:* *2,100 calories* per person per day.
  3. *Suresh Tendulkar Committee (2009):* Moved away from calorie intake to comprehensive *Monthly Per Capita Consumption Expenditure (MPCE)* covering food, education, healthcare, electricity, and clothing. For 2011–12, the Tendulkar poverty line was:
     - Rural: *₹816 per capita per month* (₹27 per day).
     - Urban: *₹1,000 per capita per month* (₹33 per day).
  4. *C. Rangarajan Committee (2014):* Revised the methodology, setting rural MPCE at *₹972 per month* and urban MPCE at *₹1,407 per month*.

== 2. Categorisation of the Poor
Development economists classify populations into distinct operational categories along the poverty continuum:

#table(
  columns: (1fr, 1.4fr, 1.6fr),
  table.header[*Broad Category*][*Sub-Categories*][*Operational Economic Profile*],
  [*1. Chronic Poor*], [
    - *Always Poor*\
    - *Usually Poor*
  ], [
    Individuals who permanently dwell below the poverty line (landless agricultural labourers, destitute urban informal workers) or spend most of their lives below it, occasionally rising slightly above during peak harvest seasons.
  ],
  [*2. Transient Poor*], [
    - *Churning Poor*\
    - *Occasionally Poor*
  ], [
    - *Churning Poor:* Regularly move in and out of poverty (small and marginal farmers, seasonal construction workers).\
    - *Occasionally Poor:* Remain above the line most of the time but fall below due to unexpected economic shocks (drought, medical catastrophe).
  ],
  [*3. Non-Poor*], [
    - *Never Poor*
  ], [
    Individuals who continuously possess sufficient wealth and steady income streams, remaining permanently above the poverty line.
  ],
)

== 3. Government Anti-Poverty Programmes (Three-Dimensional Approach)
The government's anti-poverty strategy has historically followed a three-dimensional framework:
1. *Growth-Oriented Approach:* Relying on the "trickle-down effect" of rapid GDP growth to lift living standards (proved insufficient as growth remained uneven).
2. *Direct Poverty Alleviation Programmes:*
   - *Self-Employment Programmes:*
     - *Rural Employment Generation Programme (REGP)* and *Prime Minister's Rozgar Yojana (PMRY)*: Provided subsidized bank credit to educated unemployed rural and urban youth to launch micro-enterprises.
     - *Swarna Jayanti Gram Swarozgar Yojana (SGSY)*: Launched in 1999 to organize rural poor into Self-Help Groups (SHGs); later restructured into the *National Rural Livelihood Mission (NRLM / Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana)*.
   - *Wage-Employment Programmes:*
     - *MGNREGA (Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act, 2005):* Enacts a statutory legal guarantee of at least *100 days of unskilled wage employment* per financial year to every rural household whose adult members volunteer to do manual work. At least *one-third (33%) of beneficiary jobs are legally reserved for women*.
3. *Provision of Basic Minimum Services:* Public Distribution System (PDS) for subsidized foodgrains, *Mid-Day Meal Scheme (PM POSHAN)* in schools, *National Social Assistance Programme (NSAP)* for elderly pensions, and *Pradhan Mantri Jan Dhan Yojana (PMJDY)* for universal banking inclusion.

