= Human Capital Formation (HCF): People as Resource

Human capital refers to the stock of skill, knowledge, expertise, education, and health embodied in the population of a nation at a point in time.

== 1. Physical Capital vs Human Capital
- *Physical Capital:* Tangible economic assets (machinery, factories, buildings). It is completely separable from its owner, can be traded freely in commercial markets, and depreciates continuously through physical wear, tear, and technological obsolescence.
- *Human Capital:* Intangible asset (intellect, problem-solving skills, physical health). It is *inseparable from its owner*, cannot be sold in the market (only the services of human capital can be leased), and can actually appreciate in value over time through continuous learning and specialized experience.

== 2. The Five Sources of Human Capital Formation

#table(
  columns: (1fr, 1.4fr, 1.6fr),
  table.header[*Source of HCF*][*Economic Mechanism*][*Impact on Productivity & Growth*],
  [*1. Investment in Education*], [
    Spending on schooling, vocational training, technical degrees, and higher education.
  ], [
    Expands intellectual horizons, raises technological adaptability, improves labor efficiency, and yields the highest personal and social returns on investment.
  ],
  [*2. Investment in Health*], [
    Expenditure on *preventive medicine* (vaccination), *curative medicine* (medical treatment), *social medicine* (health literacy), and clean drinking water.
  ], [
    Directly enhances the physical stamina, mental alertness, and working lifespan of the labor force, preventing productivity losses from illness and absenteeism.
  ],
  [*3. On-the-job Training*], [
    Firms training workers either in-house under supervision or off-site at specialized technical training campuses.
  ], [
    Enables workers to master specialized firm-specific machinery and workflows; firms recoup training costs by binding workers through minimum service contracts.
  ],
  [*4. Migration*], [
    Rural workers migrating to urban industrial centers, or educated engineers migrating internationally ("Brain Drain" / Global Mobility).
  ], [
    Economically rational when the expected increase in lifetime earnings substantially exceeds the economic and psychological costs of transportation and relocation.
  ],
  [*5. Investment in Information*], [
    Spending money and time to acquire data on labor market salaries, job openings, and educational institution ratings.
  ], [
    Enables optimal human capital deployment by matching worker skills to available jobs, minimizing structural frictions.
  ],
)

== 3. Human Capital vs Human Development
A vital philosophical distinction:
- *Human Capital:* Views human beings *instrumentally as a means to an end*—as productive instruments to accelerate the growth of Gross Domestic Product (GDP). Under this view, if spending on education and health does not increase labor productivity, it is deemed inefficient.
- *Human Development:* Views human beings as an *end in themselves*. Education and health are fundamental human rights and essential capabilities that every citizen is entitled to enjoy, regardless of whether it translates into higher material GDP output.

== 4. Growth of the Education Sector in India
- *Constitutional Milestones:*
  - *Article 45 (Directive Principles):* Originally urged the State to provide free and compulsory education for all children up to the age of 14 within 10 years of the Constitution's commencement.
  - *86th Constitutional Amendment Act, 2002:* Inserted *Article 21A* into the Constitution, elevating the *Right to Free and Compulsory Education* for all children aged 6 to 14 years to the status of a Fundamental Right. Enacted through the *Right to Education (RTE) Act, 2009*.
- *Public Expenditure Benchmarks:*
  - *Kothari Commission (1964–1966):* The first commission to recommend that the government allocate at least *6% of GDP* to education.
  - *Tapas Majumdar Committee (1999):* Strongly reaffirmed the necessity of dedicating 6% of GDP to achieve universal school education. Currently, actual public spending on education remains stuck at approximately *3% to 4% of GDP*.
- *Regulatory Apex Bodies:*
  - *UGC (University Grants Commission):* Regulates higher and university education.
  - *AICTE (All India Council for Technical Education):* Accreditates engineering and technical education.
  - *NCERT (National Council of Educational Research and Training):* Formulates school curriculum frameworks and textbooks.
  - *ICMR (Indian Council of Medical Research):* Guides medical research and standards.

